                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Ah yes, another one of those ultra-cheap games that seem to exist solely to fill out random bundle packs. You know the type. The kind that makes you wonder whether anyone actually tested it before release.\n\nTo be fair, Circularity isn't completely terrible. The minimalist presentation is clean enough, and the core idea of navigating levels and collecting green orbs is mildly entertaining for a while. If this were a free mobile game, I'd probably shrug and move on.\n\nThe problem is that the game constantly gets in its own way.\n\nEvery level contains three green orbs that you're supposed to collect for completion and achievements. However, the game happily lets you finish levels without collecting them all. Want to go back later and clean up missed achievements? Good luck. There is no sensible level selection screen. The levels aren't numbered, only represented by identical circles, so you'll be counting them manually like you're solving a puzzle outside the game.\n\nMissed an orb? Pause. Exit to menu. Open level select. Count circles. Pick level. Repeat.\n\nThere isn't even a quick restart button, which is particularly painful because many solutions come down to trial and error. You'll spend nearly as much time navigating menus as actually playing.\n\nThen there are the bugs.\n\nVisual glitches cause objects to appear and disappear seemingly whenever they feel like it. But the real masterpiece is Level 72. The level requires three green orbs for completion, yet only contains two as the Dev forgot to add the 3rd. That's right: the achievement is literally impossible to obtain. No Level 72 achievement. No full completion achievement. Congratulations on your permanent 98-99% completion rate, unless you want to do workarounds ofc.\n\nWhat really amazes me is how many problems managed to find their way into a game that consists of a few lines, circles, and basic physics. Reports of the broken achievement have existed for YEARS and remain unfixed.\n\nThe longer I played, the less it felt like solving puzzles and the more it felt like wrestling with poor design decisions. What began as a mildly interesting time-waster eventually became a slog of retries, menu navigation, and frustration.\n\nThere is a decent idea here somewhere, but it's buried under bugs, missing quality-of-life features, and a level of polish that feels unfinished.\n\nSave your dollar. Buy a deck of cards and play Solitaire or something instead.\n\nGameplay:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GNDjeyyJtqw",
